# The hollow choir, seen for the first time. Fired by proximity + line of
# sight (CompProperties_TSC_ProximityDialogue on TSC_ChoirBones), once per
# save. The check spot's own options - sing, read, shove - are the follow-up;
# this is only the noticing.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_ChoirSight

== start ==
The lamplight finds them all at once: skulls, stacked rank on rank from flagstone to roof, filling the gallery wall ahead.

They are not piled. They are placed - every one of them set level, every one of them facing the same way, down the passage toward the deep floor. Whoever arranged this did it slowly, and meant it.

Nobody says anything for a moment. Under the drip of the cistern water, very faint, the sound that has followed you since the stairs is still there: someone below, singing.

* "They're facing the singing." -> facing
* "Wren. Tell me this is ordinary ossuary work." -> wren
    if in_party(TSC_Npc_Bard)
* [Move on.] -> end

== facing ==
Once it is said, it cannot be unseen: eight hundred years of the dead, seated the way a congregation sits, all of them turned toward the voice on the floor below.

Not a burial. An audience. Somebody wanted the dead to hear.

* [Move on.] -> end
    do grant_xp(10)

== wren ==
Wren does not answer at once. She has gone still in the way she does when a room is a piece of music, and when she speaks it is barely above the water-noise.

"Ossuaries face the dead east, toward the morning. These face down." A pause. "It's the meter, do you see. The rows. Long, long, short. They're not stacked, they're *scored*. Somebody wrote the dead into the song."

She pulls her cloak tighter.

"I'd like to meet whoever is still singing to them. And I'd like it very much to be on our terms."

* [Move on.] -> end
    do grant_xp(15); affinity(+5)
